<CounterfeitStudy
  title="Blown Out or Raised Up"
  subtitle="Go Deeper: The Thirty-Fourth of the 38 Blessings"
  deck="A flame put out, or a body raised?"
  description="The thirty-fourth of the 38 Blessings, Nirvana, the flame blown out, set beside Scripture's hope of the dead raised up in the body."
>
    <h2>The Teaching</h2>
    <p>The thirty-fourth blessing is "Attaining Nirvana." The Pali word <em>nibbāna</em> (Sanskrit <em>nirvāṇa</em>) means "blowing out," as a flame is blown out. What goes out are the three fires of greed, hatred, and delusion, the same three the book names at its close as the causes that end the world. One who reaches Nirvana is not reborn when he dies. Theravada Buddhism, the Buddhism of Thailand, also teaches <em>anattā</em>, that there is no lasting self. The Buddha declined to say what, if anything, remains.</p>
    <h2>What the Word Says</h2>
    <p>Scripture also promises an end to suffering. "God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes; and there shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying, neither shall there be any more pain" (Revelation 21:4). It promises rest: "There remaineth therefore a rest to the people of God" (Hebrews 4:9).</p>
    <p>But the end Scripture promises is life, not extinction. "Many of them that sleep in the dust of the earth shall awake, some to everlasting life" (Daniel 12:2). "All that are in the graves shall hear his voice, and shall come forth" (John 5:28-29). The body is "sown in corruption; it is raised in incorruption" (1 Corinthians 15:42). Job expected to see Yahuah "in my flesh," and added, "whom I shall see for myself, and mine eyes shall behold, and not another" (Job 19:26-27). The self is not blown out. It is raised and made whole.</p>
    <p>Between death and that day, Scripture says the dead sleep (Daniel 12:2; John 11:11) and "know not any thing" (Ecclesiastes 9:5). They do not cycle through other lives. They wait to be woken.</p>
    <h2>Where It Turned</h2>
    <p>The turn is in the fire. In Scripture the fire belongs to Yahuah. "Our God is a consuming fire" (Hebrews 12:29), and Torah commanded that the fire on the altar "shall ever be burning upon the altar; it shall never go out" (Leviticus 6:13). The goal of Buddhism is to put the fire out. The command of Torah is to keep it burning.</p>
    <h2>The Same Turn in Christian Circles</h2>
    <p>Many Christians picture the end as souls floating in a bodiless heaven, which sits closer to Nirvana than to the resurrection. Scripture's hope is a raised body and "new heavens and a new earth, wherein dwelleth righteousness" (2 Peter 3:13), with Yahuah dwelling among His people (Revelation 21:1-3).</p>
    <h2>The Verdict</h2>
    <p>Nirvana keeps the promise of an end to suffering and empties it. Scripture keeps the man, raises him, and puts him in Yahuah's presence.</p>
    <p class="cfs-ready"><strong>Ready Answer.</strong> Nirvana is the flame blown out; Scripture's hope is the dead raised up, "some to everlasting life" (Daniel 12:2).</p>
    <p>Revelation ends with that presence, not that absence: "Behold, the tabernacle of God is with men, and he will dwell with them, and they shall be his people" (Revelation 21:3).</p>
